// 3. Measures
// Select the _Measures table, then for each block below: Home -> New measure, paste it,
// set the format string shown in the comment and the display folder named in the section header.
//
// Why REMOVEFILTERS names both segment columns: segment is sorted by segment_order, so any visual
// grouped by segment filters segment_order too. Removing only segment would leave the share at 100%.
//
// 14 measures. No calculated columns. One calculated table (Date), in 02-model.md.
//
// Measures by page (every measure on a visual is defined below):
//   Overview          Customers, Revenue, Purchase Invoices, Repeat Customers %, At-Risk Customers,
//                     At-Risk Revenue, Customer Share %, Revenue Share %, Median Days Since Last Purchase,
//                     Median Orders, Median Spend
//   Group pages (5)   Customers, Customer Share %, Revenue Share %, Median Days Since Last Purchase, Median Orders
//   Return by month   New Customers Back Next Month %, Repeat Customers %, Return Rate %
//   Helper only       Active Customers (used inside Return Rate % and New Customers Back Next Month %)


// ============================== Display folder: Customers ==============================

// Number of scored customers in the current filter. Format: #,0
Customers =
COUNTROWS ( customers )

// This group's customers as a share of all customers (the country slicer still applies). Format: 0.0%
Customer Share % =
VAR _here = [Customers]
VAR _all =
    CALCULATE ( [Customers], REMOVEFILTERS ( customers[segment], customers[segment_order] ) )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( _here, _all )

// Median days since the customer's last purchase, counted to the day after the last purchase in the data. Format: #,0
Median Days Since Last Purchase =
MEDIAN ( customers[recency_days] )

// Median number of purchase invoices per customer. Format: #,0.0
Median Orders =
MEDIAN ( customers[orders] )

// Share of customers with two or more purchase invoices. Format: 0.0%
Repeat Customers % =
VAR _repeat =
    CALCULATE ( [Customers], customers[orders] >= 2 )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( _repeat, [Customers] )

// Number of at-risk customers: good customers who stopped buying. Format: #,0
At-Risk Customers =
CALCULATE ( [Customers], customers[segment] = "At risk" )


// ============================== Display folder: Revenue ==============================

// Purchases minus returns. Format: "<client.currency> "#,0
Revenue =
SUM ( invoices[amount] )

// This group's revenue as a share of all revenue (the country slicer still applies). Format: 0.0%
Revenue Share % =
VAR _here = [Revenue]
VAR _all =
    CALCULATE ( [Revenue], REMOVEFILTERS ( customers[segment], customers[segment_order] ) )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( _here, _all )

// Number of purchase invoices; returns are not counted. Format: #,0
Purchase Invoices =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( invoices ), invoices[invoice_type] = "Purchase" )

// Median spend per customer (purchases minus returns). Format: "<client.currency> "#,0
Median Spend =
MEDIAN ( customers[spend] )

// What the at-risk customers have spent so far: the revenue at stake. Format: "<client.currency> "#,0
At-Risk Revenue =
CALCULATE ( [Revenue], customers[segment] = "At risk" )


// ============================== Display folder: Cohorts ==============================

// Customers with at least one purchase in the current filter. Format: #,0
Active Customers =
CALCULATE ( DISTINCTCOUNT ( invoices[customer_id] ), invoices[invoice_type] = "Purchase" )

// Share of a starting month's customers who bought again in a later month.
// Use with customers[cohort_month] on rows and invoices[month_offset] on columns:
// [Customers] is not filtered by month_offset (single-direction relationship), so it is the group size. Format: 0.0%
Return Rate % =
DIVIDE ( [Active Customers], [Customers] )

// Of the customers whose first purchase was after the first month and before the last month in the data,
// the share who bought again the next month. The first month is left out (it also holds older customers)
// and the last one too (no next month yet). REMOVEFILTERS () reads the whole data range, so a slicer
// does not move it. Format: 0.0%
New Customers Back Next Month % =
VAR _firstMonth =
    CALCULATE ( MIN ( customers[cohort_month] ), REMOVEFILTERS () )
VAR _lastDate =
    CALCULATE ( MAX ( invoices[invoice_date] ), REMOVEFILTERS (), invoices[invoice_type] = "Purchase" )
VAR _lastMonth =
    DATE ( YEAR ( _lastDate ), MONTH ( _lastDate ), 1 )
VAR _newCustomers =
    CALCULATETABLE (
        VALUES ( customers[customer_id] ),
        customers[cohort_month] > _firstMonth,
        customers[cohort_month] < _lastMonth
    )
VAR _backNextMonth =
    CALCULATE ( [Active Customers], _newCustomers, invoices[month_offset] = 1 )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( _backNextMonth, COUNTROWS ( _newCustomers ) )
